{
  "dom_id": "step-q31079",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Find the solution of ${x}^{2}\\ge 5x+6.$",
  "worked_solution": "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the numbers to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & \\ge 5x+6 \\\\ {x}^{2}-5x-6 & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y={x}^{2}-5x-6$ lies above the $x$-axis or touches this axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5x-6 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=-1$ and $x=6,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059052/assets/51c7484f806696105dd570684f8b194d3c1b7fc3ea27d85a1a17f53e6f7d0a64.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-5x-6\\ge 0,\n$$\n\nwe look for the part of the parabola that is above the $x$-axis or touches this axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $x\\le -1$ or $x\\ge 6.$ \n\nUsing interval notation, we write $x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [6,\\infty ).$",
